#  Wireshark Networking Basics & Packet Analysis

A beginner-friendly guide and hands-on notes for understanding **IP addresses, ports, TCP/UDP, DNS, QUIC, and HTTP analysis using Wireshark**.

This repository is intended for students and beginners in **Networking, Cybersecurity, and DevOps** who want practical exposure to packet capturing and protocol analysis.

---

## 📌 Topics Covered

* Source IP & Destination IP
* Ports and Well-Known Ports
* Network Interfaces (Linux)
* Wireshark Interface Setup
* TCP 3-Way Handshake
* UDP and DNS Traffic
* QUIC Protocol (YouTube Traffic)
* HTTP GET & POST Analysis
* Why HTTP is Insecure

---

## 🌐 Source IP & Destination IP

Every network packet has a **source IP** and a **destination IP**.

* The **source IP** shows where the packet is coming from.
* The **destination IP** shows where the packet is going.

For example:
When you open a website, your computer’s IP is the source IP, and the web server’s IP is the destination IP.

---

## 🚪 Ports

Ports are like **doors on a computer**.
They tell the operating system which service should receive the data.

For example:

* Web traffic uses port **80** for HTTP
* Secure web traffic uses port **443** for HTTPS

---

## 🔢 Port Hierarchy (Well-Known Ports)

Some ports are reserved for specific services:

| Service | Port |
| ------- | ---- |
| HTTP    | 80   |
| HTTPS   | 443  |
| FTP     | 21   |
| SSH     | 22   |
| DNS     | 53   |
| SMTP    | 25   |

---

## 🖥️ Checking Network Interfaces (Linux)

Open terminal and type:

```
ifconfig
```

You may see interfaces like:

* **elp20 / eth0 / enp0s3** → Ethernet interface (wired connection)
* **wlp1s0 / wlan0** → Wireless interface (WiFi)
* **lo** → Loopback interface (used for internal communication inside the system)
* **proton0 / tun0** → OpenVPN interface
* **wg0** → WireGuard VPN interface

> NOTE: Interface names may differ depending on operating system and distribution. These examples are from Linux.

---

## 🧰 Wireshark Interface Setup

Before capturing packets:

1. Open Wireshark
2. Right-click **Profile** → Create or select profile
3. Go to **Edit → Preferences**
4. Explore options such as:

   * Capture
   * Colors
   * Layout
   * Packet Diagram (if available)

Customize Wireshark according to your preference.

Select the interface you are connected to and start capture.

---

## 🔁 TCP (Transmission Control Protocol)

TCP is a **connection-oriented protocol**.

It uses a **3-Way Handshake** to establish a connection:

1. SYN
2. SYN-ACK
3. ACK

### Wireshark Display Filters

Show all TCP packets:

```
tcp
```

SYN packet:

```
tcp.flags.syn == 1 && tcp.flags.ack == 0
```

SYN-ACK packet:

```
tcp.flags.syn == 1 && tcp.flags.ack == 1
```

ACK packet:

```
tcp.flags.syn == 0 && tcp.flags.ack == 1
```

---

## 📦 UDP (User Datagram Protocol)

UDP is **connectionless** and does **not guarantee packet delivery**.

Used in:

* Online games
* Video calls
* Streaming

---

## 🌍 DNS Using UDP Port 53

DNS uses **UDP port 53** to resolve domain names into IP addresses.

Run in terminal:

```
nslookup google.com
```

Example Output:

```
Server: 127.0.0.53
Address: 127.0.0.53#53

Name: google.com
Address: 142.250.193.78
Name: google.com
Address: 2404:6800:4002:82a::200e
```

Wireshark Filter:

```
udp.port == 53
```

Expand:
DNS → Queries → Hostname (e.g., [www.google.com](http://www.google.com))

---

## 📺 QUIC Protocol (YouTube Traffic)

YouTube uses **QUIC**, a protocol built on top of UDP.

Features of QUIC:

* Encryption (TLS)
* Faster connection setup
* Congestion control

Wireshark Filter:

```
quic
```

QUIC prevents raw video from being sent as plain UDP, making traffic more secure and reliable.

---

## 🌐 HTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol)

HTTP is an **application-layer protocol** used for transferring web data between client and server.

### GET Request (Retrieve data)

```
http.request.method == "GET"
```

### POST Request (Send data / forms / login)

```
http.request.method == "POST"
```

---

## 🔓 Why HTTP Is Insecure

Steps:

1. Open an HTTP website (example: [https://demo.testfire.net/](https://demo.testfire.net/))
2. Go to login page
3. Enter:

   * Username: test
   * Password: 12345
4. Keep Wireshark capturing
5. Apply filter:

```
http
```

Or directly:

```
http.request.method == "POST"
```

Open the POST packet → Expand HTTP → Form Data

You will see:

```
username=test
password=12345
```

Credentials appear in **plain text**.

This proves why HTTP is insecure and why HTTPS is required.

